&lt;p&gt;In 1967, John Shepherd-Barron reached his bank minutes after closing and could not get at his own money. In the bath that evening, he thought of a chocolate bar dispenser and swapped the chocolate for cash. On 27 June, outside a Barclays branch on Enfield High Street, an actor named Reg Varney drew £10 from the world&#39;s first cash machine. It took a four-digit code (originally six, until his wife told him nobody remembers six). The bank&#39;s hours had stopped being the customer&#39;s problem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Nearly sixty years later, the bar has moved, but the test hasn&#39;t: a personal financial product is judged on whether it fits the life of the person using it. People do not read documentation, forgive a confusing screen, or care how the ledger works. They want one place that makes sense of everything — spending, saving, planning, investing — and that anticipates rather than reports: where they stand, what deserves attention, what to do next. Mercury is building personal banking* to that standard.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Personal Banking is how Mercury earns a place in someone&#39;s financial life. We&#39;re looking for a Head of Product to own it end-to-end: setting product strategy, leading a team of product managers, driving growth and engagement, and owning business performance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;*Mercury is a fintech company, not an FDIC-insured bank. Banking services provided through Choice Financial Group and Column N.A., Members FDIC.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
